# 
# https://w3id.org/eudpp#SubstanceOfConcern
<https://w3id.org/eudpp#SubstanceOfConcern> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/eudpp#SubstanceOfConcern> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/eudpp#Substance> .
<https://w3id.org/eudpp#SubstanceOfConcern> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/references> "ESPR Article 2(27)"@en .
<https://w3id.org/eudpp#SubstanceOfConcern> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/source> <http://data.europa.eu/eli/reg/2024/1781/oj> .
<https://w3id.org/eudpp#SubstanceOfConcern> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "A substance that: (a) meets the criteria laid down in Article 57 of Regulation (EC) No 1907/2006 and is identified in accordance with Article 59(1) of that Regulation; (b) is classified in Part 3 of Annex VI to Regulation (EC) No 1272/2008 in one of the following hazard classes or hazard categories: (i) carcinogenicity categories 1 and 2; (ii) germ cell mutagenicity categories 1 and 2; (iii) reproductive toxicity categories 1 and 2; (iv) endocrine disruption for human health categories 1 and 2; (v) endocrine disruption for the environment categories 1 and 2; (vi) persistent, mobile and toxic or very persistent, very mobile properties; (vii) persistent, bioaccumulative and toxic or very persistent, very bioaccumulative properties; (viii) respiratory sensitisation category 1; (ix) skin sensitisation category 1; (x) hazardous to the aquatic environment — categories chronic 1 to 4; (xi) hazardous to the ozone layer; (xii) specific target organ toxicity — repeated exposure categories 1 and 2; (xiii) specific target organ toxicity — single exposure categories 1 and 2; (c) is regulated under Regulation (EU) 2019/1021; or (d) negatively affects the reuse and recycling of materials in the product in which it is present."@en .
<https://w3id.org/eudpp#SubstanceOfConcern> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://w3id.org/eudpp/SOC> .
<https://w3id.org/eudpp#SubstanceOfConcern> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Substance of concern"@en .
<https://w3id.org/eudpp#SubstanceOfConcern> <https://w3id.org/eudpp#sourceStatus> "direct legal definition reproduced from the cited legislation."@en .
# 
# https://w3id.org/eudpp#SubstancePresence
<https://w3id.org/eudpp#SubstancePresence> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/eudpp#SubstancePresence> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/references> "ESPR Article 7(5)(a)–(e)"@en .
<https://w3id.org/eudpp#SubstancePresence> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/source> <http://data.europa.eu/eli/reg/2024/1781/oj> .
<https://w3id.org/eudpp#SubstancePresence> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "A contextual relation entity representing the presence of a substance in relation to a product as placed on the market, including a product, component or spare part. It enables product-dependent location, concentration and instruction information to be represented without treating those data as intrinsic characteristics of the substance itself or classifying the presence as a product property. In the CORE profile the modelling pattern is one SubstancePresence per reported location when different locations require different concentrations; sector-specific extensions may provide explicit reference bases for several calculation perspectives. This class is an ontology modelling abstraction supporting ESPR Article 7(5), rather than a term explicitly defined by the Regulation."@en .
<https://w3id.org/eudpp#SubstancePresence> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> <https://w3id.org/eudpp/SOC> .
<https://w3id.org/eudpp#SubstancePresence> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#isDefinedBy> "https://w3id.org/eudpp/SOC" .
<https://w3id.org/eudpp#SubstancePresence> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Substance presence"@en .
<https://w3id.org/eudpp#SubstancePresence> <https://w3id.org/eudpp#sourceStatus> "ESPR-grounded ontology modelling abstraction; this RDF resource is not itself a term defined by the Regulation."@en .
# 
# https://w3id.org/eudpp#SustainableRenewableMaterialsUseResult
<https://w3id.org/eudpp#SustainableRenewableMaterialsUseResult> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/eudpp#SustainableRenewableMaterialsUseResult> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/eudpp#InventoryIndicatorResult> .
<https://w3id.org/eudpp#SustainableRenewableMaterialsUseResult> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "An LCA inventory indicator result quantifying the use of renewable secondary material and fuel inputs in a product system. \nCommonly associated with the EN 15804+A2 inventory indicators “Use of renewable secondary fuels” (RSF) or “Use of non-renewable secondary fuels” (NRSF), though no formal quantifiesIndicator restriction is imposed, as the scope of renewable material inputs may evolve."@en .
<https://w3id.org/eudpp#SustainableRenewableMaterialsUseResult> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Sustainable Renewable Materials Use Result"@en .
<https://w3id.org/eudpp#SustainableRenewableMaterialsUseResult> <https://w3id.org/eudpp#sourceVocabulary> "LCA ontology"@en .
# 
# https://w3id.org/eudpp#TechnicalSpecification
<https://w3id.org/eudpp#TechnicalSpecification> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/eudpp#TechnicalSpecification> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/eudpp#ProductRequirement> .
<https://w3id.org/eudpp#TechnicalSpecification> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/source> "Regulation (EU) No 1025/2012 Art 2 (4)\nhttps://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=celex%3A32012R1025#art_2"@en .
<https://w3id.org/eudpp#TechnicalSpecification> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Technical specification means a document that prescribes technical requirements to be fulfilled by a product, process, service or system and which lays down one or more of the following: Common Specification or Other Technical Specification."@en .
<https://w3id.org/eudpp#TechnicalSpecification> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Technical Specification"@en .
# 
# https://w3id.org/eudpp#ThresholdOfSubstanceOfConcern
<https://w3id.org/eudpp#ThresholdOfSubstanceOfConcern> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Class> .
<https://w3id.org/eudpp#ThresholdOfSubstanceOfConcern> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <https://w3id.org/eudpp#QuantitativeProperty> .
<https://w3id.org/eudpp#ThresholdOfSubstanceOfConcern> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/references> "ESPR FAQ 10.115, p59"@en .
<https://w3id.org/eudpp#ThresholdOfSubstanceOfConcern> <http://purl.org/dc/terms/source> "https://circabc.europa.eu/ui/group/418195ae-4919-45fa-a959-3b695c9aab28/library/25c48e7c-9ce3-41cb-96ac-d2942a8a29d6/details" .
<https://w3id.org/eudpp#ThresholdOfSubstanceOfConcern> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Relevant threshold of Substance of Concern.\n\nBy default, information on all substances of concern present in a product, above the  relevant thresholds, should be included in the DPP [ESPR FAQ 10.115, p59]"@en .
<https://w3id.org/eudpp#ThresholdOfSubstanceOfConcern> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Threshold of substance of concern"@en .
